## 1. Health check endpoint (`/up`)

Rails 7.1+ ships this generator, but here's the full setup so Kamal's healthcheck works.

**`config/routes.rb`**
```ruby
Rails.application.routes.draw do
  # Kamal / load balancer health probe — no auth, no DB dependency for liveness
  get "up" => "rails/health#show", as: :rails_health_check

  # Optional: a deeper readiness probe that DOES check DB + Redis
  get "ready" => "health#ready"

  # ...your other routes
end
```

**`app/controllers/health_controller.rb`**
```ruby
class HealthController < ActionController::API
  def ready
    checks = {
      database: database_ok?,
      redis:    redis_ok?,
      migrations: migrations_ok?
    }

    if checks.values.all?
      render json: { status: "ok", checks: checks }, status: :ok
    else
      render json: { status: "degraded", checks: checks }, status: :service_unavailable
    end
  end

  private

  def database_ok?
    ActiveRecord::Base.connection.execute("SELECT 1")
    true
  rescue StandardError
    false
  end

  def redis_ok?
    return true unless defined?(Redis)
    Redis.new(url: ENV["REDIS_URL"]).ping == "PONG"
  rescue StandardError
    false
  end

  def migrations_ok?
    !ActiveRecord::Base.connection.migration_context.needs_migration?
  rescue StandardError
    false
  end
end
```

Point Kamal's `healthcheck.path` at `/up` for liveness (fast, no dependencies) and optionally use `/ready` in your load balancer. If you're on Rails < 7.1, replace `rails/health#show` with `health#show` and add a `show` action that returns `head :ok`.

